{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. It focuses on small-probability outcomes (less than 20%) and adjusts their weights upward by 10 percentage points. In Lottery A, the outcomes are: 4000€ (25%), 2000€ (25%), and 0€ (50%). None of the non-zero outcomes in A have probabilities below 20%, so no weighting adjustment applies to A. In Lottery B, the outcome is 6000€ (25%) and 0€ (75%). Similarly, the 25% probability is not below 20%, so no outcome in B qualifies for the probability weighting adjustment. Therefore, even though the rule is defined, no outcome in either lottery meets the 'less than 20%' threshold, so the adjusted weighting does not alter the original probabilities for any outcome. The reason would thus reduce to comparing expected values of the small-probability outcomes—but since no outcomes fall below 20%, the set of such outcomes is empty for both lotteries. As a result, the reason cannot differentiate between A and B based on its rule and would be indifferent.",
